  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Severe thunderstorms capable of damaging winds \nand heavy rainfall remain possible today into this evening. An\nisolated tornado will also be possible.\n\nCurrent radar outlines a cluster of showers and thunderstorms \ngenerally south of a FIG-UNV-AVP line, generally moving east- \nsoutheastward as we move into the mid-afternoon hours. Some \nbreaks in the clouds during the late morning and early afternoon\nhours has allowed for destabilization across this region, which\nwill allow for some severe potential throughout the rest of the\nevening hours. Recent SPC Mesoanalysis maximized SBCAPE in the \n1500-2000 J/kg range across southeastern Pennsylvania, which is \nmore than sufficient for some strong-to-severe thunderstorms in \nthe near-term. Shear remains the main question mark with regards\nto a more organized severe threat with recent bulk shear values\ntopping out closer to 20 knots across south-central PA. Despite\nthis lack of shear, damaging winds will remain possible over \nthe next couple of hours and into the evening as more shear is \npresent closer to southeastern Pennsylvania. Of note, 0-1 km \nshear on most recent SPC Mesoanalysis appears to be sufficient \nfor some tornadic potential as storms move further southeast \ninto slightly more favorable shear. In fact, the best storm (as \nof 1PM EDT) across Huntingdon County did show some history of \nrotation, and will be moving into a slightly more favorable \nenvironment this afternoon. Given all these factors, Severe \nThunderstorm Watch will be in effect for portions of southern \nand eastern Pennsylvania until 8PM this evening.\n\nHeavy rainfall signal remains concerning for much of central \nPennsylvania with some gauge reports in Clearfield County and \nCambria County reporting fairly widespread 3-5\" and have \nprompted two Flash Flood Warnings this afternoon. HREF PWATs \nnear the 90th percentile will warrant some watching of areas \nthat receive either multiple rounds of thunderstorms and/or \nslow-moving thunderstorms this evening. Overall, cells appear \nthat they will be moving, thus locations would ultimately need \nto receive multiple rounds of this heavy rainfall in order for \nproblems to arise. That being said, instantaneous rainfall rates\nbetween 2-4\"/hr could cause some issues in the more urbanized \nareas of southeastern Pennsylvania and the rolling hills of \nsouth-central Pennsylvania where water will drain towards the \nlowest locations. Thus, have expanded the Flood Watch a tier of \ncounties northward and eastward to grab the south-central \nmountains to cover this threat.\n\nGiven the dual hydrology and severe threat this evening, have \nmultiple ways to receive warnings! If outdoors this afternoon \nand evening, continue to monitor your local forecasts and have \nplans in case a severe storms and/or flooding occurs. Some \nstorms will produce damaging winds and/or potentially a tornado \nthis afternoon/evening, while some will not. Flooding will also \nbe possible this evening given the ample rainfall rates, so if \nyou are located near smaller streams and/or urbanized areas, be \nsure to monitor nearby surroundings if a thunderstorm moves over\nyour area.",
